# Lane notes: N14 (maths font throughout + dark mode)

## What was built

- One colour table: `mpp-ide/src/theme/palette.rs` (`Palette::LIGHT`, `Palette::DARK`). It wraps the
  render `Theme` (page, ink, results, ghost results, placeholder outlines, error/warning/info, graph
  colours) and adds the interface colours (panel, widgets, border, tooltip, selection, selected row,
  highlight, scrollbar, edge). A test scans `mpp-ide/src` and fails on any colour literal outside `theme/`.
- Fonts: `theme/fonts.rs`. Latin Modern Roman is the Proportional family (every part of the UI),
  Latin Modern Math is its fallback, Latin Modern Sans is the family `theme::sans(size)` (used for the
  status bar only; at 14 px it reads better than Roman, decided from the screenshots), Latin Modern Mono is
  the Monospace family and only the View, Show file text pop-out uses it. Fonts live in
  `crates/mpp-render/fonts` with the GUST licence; mpp-render exports `LM_SANS` and `LM_MONO`.
- Theme settings: `ThemeChoice` (Light, Dark, Follow Windows; default Follow Windows), saved under the
  key `theme` (the old `dark` key is still read). View, Theme sub-menu; a moon button in the status bar
  (drawn as a shape, because Latin Modern and egui's default fonts have no moon glyph); chord
  Ctrl+K then Ctrl+T cycles Light, Dark, Follow Windows. `IdeApp::mode` is now the resolved mode, set every
  frame from `theme_choice` and the system theme; call `set_theme_choice` instead of writing `mode`.
- Dark-mode ink bug: maths and prose were the same colour, but light hairline glyphs on a dark page look thinner.
  `paint_glyph` now thickens the glyph edge in dark mode, so maths matches the text weight.
- egui keeps one text style per theme; `install_fonts` now sets sizes for both (dark mode had been using
  egui defaults).
- Contrast: `palette.rs` tests check every text pair at 4.5:1 and every line pair at 3:1 in both themes.
  Light ghost results, placeholder outline and label, and the warning colour were darkened to pass.

## For the plot lane (M13): palette requirements

Take everything from `mpp_render::Theme` (passed in as `&Theme`); never name a colour.

- Figure background: `theme.background` (the page), or `theme.code_background` for a boxed figure.
- Grid lines: `theme.grid` (quiet, 1.2:1 or better against the background, always quieter than the axes).
- Axes, ticks and frame: `theme.axis` (3:1 or better; ink-like).
- Axis labels, tick labels, legend text and titles: `theme.text` (4.5:1), set in Latin Modern Roman,
  with maths labels in Latin Modern Math.
- Curves: `theme.series[i % 8]`, in order. All eight are 3:1 or better against `theme.background` in both
  themes and are chosen for dark backgrounds in `Theme::DARK` (lighter tints). Use a line width of at
  least 1.5 px, and a dash or marker as well as colour when more than four curves share a plot.
- 3D surfaces: colour maps should run from `series[0]` through `series[4]` or a perceptual map whose
  ends contrast with `theme.background`; wire frames use `theme.axis`; the plane under the surface uses `theme.grid`.
- Legend and annotation boxes: `theme.code_background` fill, `theme.rule` outline.
- Errors drawn on a plot (a gap, a domain error): `theme.error`; warnings `theme.warning`.
- Export (N8) draws with `Theme::LIGHT` unless the user asks for dark.

## Note for N8 (export)

Export stays light by default: call the renderers with `Theme::LIGHT` regardless of the IDE mode, with an
"export in dark" option that passes `Theme::DARK`. The fonts are the same Latin Modern files
(embed Roman, Math, and Sans only if a figure uses it). Also embed `GUST-FONT-LICENSE.txt`.

## Shared-file requests for the integrator

- None: no new dependencies, and no change to Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, the grammar or the guide.
- docs/guide.mpp: a short "Theme" paragraph: View, Theme (Light, Dark, Follow Windows), the moon button in
  the status bar, and Ctrl+K then Ctrl+T.

## Screenshots

`cargo test -p mpp-ide --test ui` writes ide-light/dark, ide-errors-light/dark and
ide-completion-light/dark PNGs to `target/ide-snapshots/`.
